## Included, or derived from

Linux kernel, exynos850-mainline tree (GPL-2.0-only)
  https://github.com/exynos850-mainline/linux, branch DNM/scsc-wlan. The sources are fetched by
  scripts/prepare_env.sh at a pinned commit and are not in this repository. The files in kernel/
  and mangos/tools/ are written for the Linux kernel and are GPL-2.0-only. The files in
  kernel-patches/ are patches to that tree and carry its license. The Samsung Wi-Fi driver (SCSC)
  that some of them modify is in that tree.

uniLoader (GPL-2.0)
  https://github.com/exynos850-mainline/uniLoader, fork of https://github.com/ivoszbg/uniLoader.
  Fetched at a pinned commit by scripts/prepare_env.sh. It carries the kernel in the boot image.
  The README of the project states GPLv2; the license file was not read.

Drivers derived from Samsung and Himax sources (GPL-2.0-only)
  kernel/drivers/power/supply/sm5714.c, kernel/drivers/video/backlight/s2dps01.c and
  mangos/tools/{abox,chub,camera,acpm_gpu,acpm_thermal,reboot_target} were written from the
  register layouts, sequences and conversion formulas in the Samsung kernel source for the Exynos
  850 devices (Samsung Electronics, kernel 4.19, published under GPL-2.0). The touchscreen driver
  kernel/drivers/input/touchscreen/himax_hx83112f.c takes its registers, commands and sequences
  from the "Himax Android Driver Sample Code" in the Samsung SM-M135F kernel source
  (Copyright (C) 2019 Himax Corporation, GPL-2.0).

Camera register tables (GPL-2.0-only)
  mangos/tools/camera/s5kjn1_tables.h is generated by scripts/camera_tables.py from
  drivers/media/i2c/s5kjn1.c of the Linux kernel (Copyright (c) 2025 Linaro Ltd).
  mangos/tools/camera/gc08a3_tables.h is generated from drivers/media/i2c/gc08a3.c
  (Copyright (c) 2024 MediaTek Inc.). Both files say so in their headers.

Kernel configuration (GPL-2.0)
  kernel-kconfig/config-postmarketos-exynos850.aarch64 derives from the kernel configuration of the
  postmarketOS (now Nura) package linux-postmarketos-exynos850 for Exynos 850 devices, based on
  the exynos850_defconfig of the exynos850-mainline project.

Device tree (GPL-2.0-only)
  kernel/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos850-m135f.dts includes the A135F device tree of the
  exynos850-mainline tree and adds what the M13 board needs.

three.js r128 (MIT)
  https://threejs.org, loaded from cdnjs by branding/laptop/laptop.html. It is only the tool that
  renders mangos/web/laptop.png on the PC; it is not part of the system and is not included.

## Downloaded, not included

Alpine Linux 3.24 (various licenses, mostly GPL, MIT and BSD)
  https://alpinelinux.org. The base system and the packages listed in scripts/common.sh come from
  Alpine's repositories under their own licenses, among them busybox and e2fsprogs (GPL-2.0, also
  embedded in the boot ramdisk built by the scripts), Chromium, cage, Mesa, onnxruntime and
  espeak-ng (GPL-3.0-or-later, not verified).

Inter font (SIL Open Font License 1.1)
  https://github.com/rsms/inter, release 4.1, Copyright (c) 2016 The Inter Project Authors. Used
  by the UI and the boot screen. Verified on the project's LICENSE.txt.

Parakeet TDT 0.6B v3 (CC-BY-4.0)
  Model by NVIDIA, https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v3. mangOS uses the ONNX
  conversion by istupakov, https://huggingface.co/istupakov/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v3-onnx, also
  CC-BY-4.0 (both verified on the model pages). scripts/parakeet_mixed.py rebuilds the encoder of
  that model with part of it in floating point. The rebuilt encoder is a derivative: attribution
  to NVIDIA applies.

onnx-asr (MIT)
  https://github.com/istupakov/onnx-asr, version 0.12.0 from PyPI. Verified on the project page.

Piper voice it_IT-paola-medium: for research and non-commercial use only
  https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices (the repository metadata says MIT; Piper itself is
  not used, only the voice model, run by our own code with onnxruntime). The voice model card says
  it was trained on the dataset https://huggingface.co/datasets/paolapersico1/Voice-Dataset-Italian
  (CC0-1.0, verified) and "finetuned from U.S. English lessac voice (medium quality)". The lessac
  voice comes from the Blizzard 2013 Lessac dataset, whose license
  (https://www.cstr.ed.ac.uk/projects/blizzard/2013/lessac_blizzard2013/license.html) is limited to
  research purposes: it excludes commercial use and redistribution, as the page reads. How this
  affects a model fine-tuned from it is not clear, and was not resolved. Check it before any
  commercial use, or use another voice. The voice is not included in this repository.

Heimdall (MIT-style license)
  The grimler fork, https://git.sr.ht/~grimler/Heimdall, commit pinned in scripts/prepare_env.sh.
  Heimdall is Copyright 2010-2017 Benjamin Dobell and Glass Echidna (checked on the upstream
  project at https://github.com/Benjamin-Dobell/Heimdall; the license file of the fork itself was
  not read). Built on your PC by scripts/prepare_env.sh to write partitions in download mode.

postmarketOS (now Nura) A13 images
  scripts/prepare_env.sh downloads the reference A13 boot image from
  https://github.com/exynos850-mainline/releases. The system image used as the fallback system is
  not downloaded by the script: you download it from the same release (docs/PORTING.md, step 6).
  They are used to take the boot header format and the partition layout, and are not included.
  License terms of the images were not verified.

Claude Code (proprietary, Anthropic)
  Installed from npm (@anthropic-ai/claude-code) at the version pinned in scripts/common.sh, under
  Anthropic's terms, and used with your own Claude subscription. It is not included. Claude and
  Claude Code are trademarks of Anthropic.

Deepgram (cloud service)
  With `pim speech cloud`, speech is transcribed and spoken by Deepgram's Nova-3 and Aura-2 over
  the network, with your own key and under Deepgram's terms. Nothing of Deepgram is included.

## Proprietary, not included

Samsung firmware
  The touchscreen firmware, the Wi-Fi firmware, the audio processor firmware and the sensor
  microcontroller firmware are Samsung's proprietary firmware. They are not in this repository and
  must not be added to it. You extract them from your own phone (docs/PORTING.md, step 2). Samsung
  and Galaxy are trademarks of Samsung Electronics.

  The images and archives you build contain that firmware: the boot image has the touchscreen
  firmware inside the kernel, and the system archive has the Wi-Fi, audio and sensor firmware.
  They cannot be redistributed. Publish the source and the scripts, not the build.

The Samsung bootloader and security firmware stay on the phone. They are signed and are not
replaced.
